      "transcript_text": "Okay, now the question is why do firms invest in real assets? The answer is firms invest in real assets for producing goods and services. These goods and services are in turn used to generate income. Now the question is these real assets are not going to drop off from the sky. You need to fund your investment in real assets. So the question before us is from where do firms or corporations get money to fund or finance their investments in real assets? Okay, there are a number of ways. For example, if the company or the company has its operations and if it already has some cash reserves, it can make use of those cash reserves to fund the investment in real assets. Alternatively, the company can borrow money from a bank or a financial institutions. Yet another an alternative is that a company can borrow money from the public at large by issuing them bonds. and yet another alternative could be is that a company can issue additional shares and raise money. Okay, so therefore a firm's financial manager faces two broad financial questions. The first one is what kind of investments should the company make which is our investment decision? And the second one is the financial investment decision. So this is how should the company pay for those investments which is our financing decision? So this subject of corporate finance is primarily about investment and financing decisions. So investment decision is about spending money for the purchase of real assets and financing decision is about raising money from the sale of financial assets. Having said that, investment decisions are not only about purchase of real assets. They are also about managing the assets which are already in place and then disposing them of once they have lived off their utility. In a similar way, financing decisions are not only about raising money. They are also about meeting obligations which arise as a consequence of raising money. If any of you have taken loans in the past, think about it. When you take a loan, it does not mean that your transaction has ended then and there. You need to repay the loan along with interest over the term of the loan. So, this is also a part of your financing decision. For example, investment decision, meta acquired WhatsApp for $20 billion in 2014. What is it? It is an investment decision. It is an inorganic investment made by meta. How did meta fund for this investment? Meta paid for this investment through cash and its own shares. So, that was essentially a financing decision.",
