          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the lecture transcript, explain the two broad financial questions that corporate finance primarily addresses. Provide a real-world example from the lecture to illustrate how a single business decision involves both questions.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The two broad financial questions are: (1) The investment decision: what kind of investments should the company make, specifically spending money for the purchase of real assets, and (2) The financing decision: how should the company pay for those investments.\n\nThe lecture emphasizes that investment decisions are not only about the purchase of real assets but also about managing the assets already in place and disposing of them once they have lived off their utility. Similarly, financing decisions are not only about raising money but also about meeting obligations which arise as a consequence of raising money (e.g., repaying a loan along with interest over the term of the loan).\n\n**Real-world example**: Meta's acquisition of WhatsApp for $20 billion in 2014. This was an investment decision (inorganic investment) and a financing decision (funded through cash and Meta's own shares).\n\n**Marking Rubric**:\n- Core conceptual understanding of investment vs. financing decisions: 4 marks\n- Correct identification of the scope of each decision (management/disposal for investment; obligation repayment for financing): 3 marks\n- Correct application of the Meta/WhatsApp example to illustrate the nexus: 3 marks",
