      "transcript_text": "I have another follow-up question for you. My question for you is, is shareholder wealth maximisation strictly same as profit maximization? Many of you must be thinking about it. Is shareholder wealth maximization same as profit maximization? The answer is not really. But why? Okay, before we move in there, let me talk about two kinds of variables. Stock variables and flow variables. What are stock variables? Stock variables are those variables which are measured at a particular point in time. Remember, at a particular point in time, for example, if we talk about wealth, what is wealth? Wealth is measured at a particular point in time. You must have observed newspapers often talking about, Gotham Adhan is wealth. Mukesham Ban is wealth. And their wealth as you understand will keep changing every day. So, if you have a question for your and since wealth is measured at a particular point in time, therefore wealth is a stock variable. Now, let us talk about a flow variable. A flow variable is measured for a period of time. Let us think about a flow variable. For example, if you talk about income or profit. Income or profit is measured for a water flow period of time and therefore income or profit is a flow variable. Just think about all your balance sheet variables. Do you remember your balance sheet from financial accounting course? So balance sheet has assets, balance sheet has liabilities, balance sheet has equity. All your balance sheet variables are essentially stock variables and therefore when you look at a balance sheet it often says balance sheet as of as of 31st March 2026. It means that all the balance sheet variables are stock variables. On the other hand if we talk about income statement or profit and loss statement, all the variables of a profit and loss statement or income statement are essentially flow variables. And that is why if you read an income statement you will read at the top that income statement for the year for the year right and therefore all the income statement variables are flow variables. Now therefore as you can figure out maximizing a stock variable may not be strictly same as maximizing a flow variable. If I ask you a question suppose I tell managers that their goal is to maximize current tiers profit. Is it necessarily a good objective? The answer is no. It is not necessarily a good objective but why? Managers can maximize current tiers profit how for example by cutting down on R&D expenses by cutting down on employee training expenses. What will happen if your expenses go down? If your expenses go down in the current period your current tiers profit will go up but what will happen to your long term profit? Your long term profit as a consequence will come down because you have cut down on your employee expense you have cut down on your R&D expense. Therefore maximizing current tiers profit is not a good objective function. In other words maximizing of shareholder wealth is not strictly same as profit maximization.",
