      "transcript_text": "What is the opportunity cost of taking up the project? Is it R1? Is it R2? Or is it something else? The answer is it is R2. Why is R2? The opportunity cost of investment for this project? Just think about the definition of opportunity cost. What is the benefit that shareholders are foregoing? If the financial manager decides to invest cash in the project, clearly shareholders are foregoing the opportunity to earn a return of R2 on their own if the financial manager decides to invest cash in the project. Therefore, R2 is the opportunity cost of capital for this project. Let us define opportunity cost for a project in the language of finance. What is opportunity cost? Opportunity cost for a project is the expected return in the financial market at the same level of risk as your project. Remember, in the financial market, more risky assets are expected to give higher returns. For example, investment in stocks is expected to give a higher return than investment in bonds. Why is it so? Because investors are not going to be able to get a higher return. Investors do not just care about return, they also care about risk. The greater the risk of an investment, the greater the return required by investors from the investment. Since stocks are more risky relative to bonds, therefore, investors require a greater return from stocks than they require from bonds. What does the opportunity cost of a project? Therefore, depend on. It clearly therefore depends on the risk of the project. The greater the risk of the project, the higher the opportunity cost of capital for the project. Now, suppose there is a project and this project is a completely safe project. What do you think its opportunity cost will be? To find out the opportunity cost for this completely safe project, you will need to look to the financial markets. You need to look at those assets in the financial market which carry the same risk as the risk of your project. Whatever is the return on those assets in the financial market which carry the same risk as the risk of your project, the return on those assets essentially becomes the opportunity cost of your project. Which asset is the financial market? Do you think is the safest asset? It is the government bonds. Why? Because the government is the last entity to default on its obligations. Even if the government does not have enough money, then also the government can essentially print more money and service its obligations. In other words, since government bonds are the safest assets in the financial market, whatever is the interest rate on the government bonds, that is going to be your opportunity cost for the safest project. You might have a question. How would you know the opportunity cost for a project that is somewhat risky? You would need to apply a model called as capital asset pricing model or CAPM to know the opportunity cost for a project that is risky. Of course, we will cover this model in later modules. However, let me give you an intuition behind this model. This model says opportunity cost for a project equals risk free rate plus risk premium. What does this risk premium depend on? This risk premium clearly depends on the risk. The higher the risk, the greater the risk premium is going to be and therefore the higher is going to be your opportunity cost. This risk is represented by beta. So, if the project is not risky, the risk premium is going to be zero and therefore your opportunity cost is simply going to be equal to risk free rate, which is the interest rate on government bonds.",
