      "transcript_text": "Now, can you think of some ways as to how we can mitigate agency problems or agency costs? What can be done to mitigate agency problems? We can have some mechanisms for aligning managers objectives with those of shareholders. Alternatively, managers can also be given with employee stock options. Why? Because the greater the stock price, the greater the value of the employee stock options. So, even in this case, managers will try to maximize the stock price. Yet another mechanism to mitigate agency cost could be through monitoring of managers. You could monitor managers by supervision of board of directors. You could also monitor managers through auditors. All these mechanisms sometimes are mandatory by law. Other than these mechanisms of aligning the interest of managers with those of shareholders and monitoring them, managers can also be unleashed for their actions. Managers can be replaced if they do not pursue shareholder goals. In fact, the market itself plays a disciplining role. On this note, let me ask you one question. What kind of firms do you think are likely to be attractive targets of hostile takeovers? You understand, a hostile takeover is a takeover of a company against the wishes of its management. So, my question is, what kind of companies are more likely to become attractive targets of hostile takeovers? Often, those companies whose managers are not maximizing their value are likely to become attractive targets of hostile takeovers. In other words, the market itself plays a disciplining role and disciplines, managers. Also, managers can be put behind bars if they are not paying in some thing, which is not permitted by law. For example, managers cannot exploit the first private information they have for their personal gains. They cannot trade on unpublished price sensitive information. Trading on unpublished price sensitive information is considered as insider trading and it is prohibited by law. So, we do have mechanisms which punish managers if they indulge in self-dealing, if they indulge in something which they are not supposed to do. So, all these mechanisms that we have talked about, they form a part of a good corporate governance system.",
