          "question": "Based on the module overview, explain the sequential order of topics covered in this corporate finance module and why the separation of ownership and management is a critical starting point for understanding agency costs.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The module follows a specific sequential roadmap beginning with the introduction of two important financial principles. Subsequently, the lecture moves to discuss different forms into which a business is organized. Following this, the discussion shifts to the investment and financing decisions undertaken by companies. Thereafter, the focus turns to the financial goal of the firm or corporation. The topic then progresses to how to evaluate investment decisions, and the module concludes with a discussion on agency costs.\n\nThe separation of ownership and management is a critical starting point because, as illustrated by the professor's example of Infosys having over 2 million shareholders holding over 4 billion shares, the owners (shareholders) are too numerous to manage daily operations. This delegation of authority creates the principal-agent relationship. When the interests of the managers (agents) diverge from the wealth maximization goals of the owners (principals), agency costs arise. Understanding this separation is essential for grasping why the module ends with a discussion on agency costs, as it provides the foundational friction point that the rest of the module's evaluations and decisions must account for.",
