          "id": 1,
          "question": "Compare and contrast the features of a sole proprietorship and a partnership, focusing on ownership structure, liability, and business life. Use real-world examples from the lecture to support your answer.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "**Ownership Structure**: A sole proprietorship has a single owner, termed the proprietor, while a partnership requires a minimum of two and a maximum of fifty partners. The professor illustrated the sole proprietorship concept with the example of a freelancer, who is essentially a sole proprietor. In contrast, partnerships are often formed by individuals who know each other intimately.\n\n**Liability**: Both forms of business have unlimited liability. This means that the owners, the proprietor and the partners, are not only liable for the amount of wealth they contributed at the start of the business, but their personal wealth is also at stake. If the business is unable to repay its debts, personal assets can be seized. The professor used the example of the Tyco International CEO spending $2M on his wife's birthday party and billing $1M to the company as a direct agency cost, highlighting the personal financial risk involved in these structures.\n\n**Business Life**: Both sole proprietorships and partnerships have limited life. The business comes to an end if the sole proprietor dies or if any of the partners dies. The professor emphasized this by stating: \"These businesses come to an end if the sole proprietor or any of the partners dies.\"\n\n**Regulation**: Partnerships in India are regulated under the Indian Partnership Act 1932, whereas sole proprietorships operate without specific statutory regulation.\n\n**Key Distinction**: While both share unlimited liability and limited life, the primary difference lies in the number of owners and the specific legal framework governing the entity in India.",
